sections.s2_technical:
An enum is declared with the `enum` keyword. The simplest form is just a list of names, but the real power appears when you attach data. You add fields, a constructor, and methods exactly like a normal class, and then each constant calls the constructor with its own arguments.

The constructor of an enum is always effectively private. You cannot call `new` on an enum from outside, which is what guarantees the fixed set. The method `values()` returns an array of all constants, and `valueOf(String)` converts a name back into the constant. For example, `AccountStatus.valueOf("ACTIVE")` returns the ACTIVE constant, and `AccountStatus.values().length` tells you there are three statuses.

Each constant is a singleton, so you can safely compare enums with `==` instead of `.equals()`. For example `if (account.getStatus() == AccountStatus.FROZEN)` is both faster and null-safe compared to String comparison. The method `name()` returns the exact declared name as a String, and `ordinal()` returns the zero-based position. A senior tip is to never persist `ordinal()` to a database, because reordering constants later silently corrupts stored data; persist `name()` instead.

Enums can implement interfaces, which lets them plug into existing designs. For instance an enum `TransactionType` could implement a `FeeCalculator` interface so each type computes its own fee. Enums can also have fields with behavior; here `AccountStatus` carries a `withdrawAllowed` boolean and a human-readable `label`, and exposes `canWithdraw()`:

```java
public enum AccountStatus {
    ACTIVE("Active account", true),
    FROZEN("Frozen by compliance", false),
    CLOSED("Closed permanently", false);

    private final String label;
    private final boolean withdrawAllowed;

    AccountStatus(String label, boolean withdrawAllowed) {
        this.label = label;
        this.withdrawAllowed = withdrawAllowed;
    }

    public String label() { return label; }
    public boolean canWithdraw() { return withdrawAllowed; }
}
```

Because enums are created once and are immutable if you keep their fields final, they are inherently thread-safe, which matters a lot in a concurrent banking system where many threads read the same status constant.

